workedExamples.question_text:
Each shape contains separate matchstick squares. Shapes $1$, $2$ and $3$ are shown. Each line segment represents one match.
Write an equation for the number of matches $y$ in shape $x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
\node at (0.5,-0.5) {$1$};
\draw (2,0) rectangle (3,1);
\draw (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,1);
\node at (3.2,-0.5) {$2$};
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.4,1);
\draw (6.8,0) rectangle (7.8,1);
\draw (8.2,0) rectangle (9.2,1);
\node at (7.3,-0.5) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A matchstick pattern has three rows of squares. Each new shape adds one column, and neighbouring squares share sides. Shapes $1$, $2$ and $3$ are shown. Each segment between neighbouring grid intersections represents one match.
Write an equation for the number of matches $y$ in shape $x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\foreach \r in {0,1,2,3} {\draw (0,\r)--(1,\r);}
\foreach \c in {0,1} {\draw (\c,0)--(\c,3);}
\node at (0.5,-0.5) {$1$};
\foreach \r in {0,1,2,3} {\draw (2.5,\r)--(4.5,\r);}
\foreach \c in {0,1,2} {\draw ({2.5+\c},0)--({2.5+\c},3);}
\node at (3.5,-0.5) {$2$};
\foreach \r in {0,1,2,3} {\draw (6,\r)--(9,\r);}
\foreach \c in {0,1,2,3} {\draw ({6+\c},0)--({6+\c},3);}
\node at (7.5,-0.5) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

